1
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:08,760
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:12,161
Last time, we got this far with the first-pass coloring.

3
00:00:13,725 --> 00:00:21,320
Before proceeding further, let me briefly cover the glazing coloring method from Chapter 3, part 3, as a review.

4
00:00:22,945 --> 00:00:27,900
First, for comparison, I'll shrink the picture I drew last time and put it up next to me.

5
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,561
Then I'll color it with glazing from the start.

6
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:43,840
First, let me lay down the base color as a single color.

7
00:00:43,841 --> 00:00:50,281
Then, after rendering it in grayscale, I'll composite the base color layer using the layer property.

8
00:00:56,835 --> 00:00:59,150
I said inherent value is important.

9
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:02,990
Before dividing the value steps, let me set the inherent value first.

10
00:01:04,834 --> 00:01:09,310
Since there's already a base color set, setting the inherent value isn't hard.

11
00:01:10,869 --> 00:01:17,560
It's easier to just convert the base color to grayscale, but for the sake of learning, I painted it like this.

12
00:01:23,294 --> 00:01:27,171
Now I'll render it in grayscale.

13
00:01:41,774 --> 00:01:43,790
I've done about five minutes.

14
00:01:44,674 --> 00:01:49,940
Now let me raise the base color layer by changing its property to color.

15
00:02:03,184 --> 00:02:10,770
The clothing seems too dark, so I'll brighten the grayscale layer of the clothing a bit.

16
00:02:19,165 --> 00:02:20,040
How is it?

17
00:02:20,041 --> 00:02:23,080
The first-pass coloring finished too simply.

18
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:28,040
The advantage of this glazing coloring method is that you can work fast.

19
00:02:28,041 --> 00:02:34,360
However, there's the difficulty of having to keep the inherent value, so for beginners it may not be very effective.

20
00:02:35,744 --> 00:02:39,100
Let me compare by breaking the inherent value.

21
00:02:39,101 --> 00:02:43,420
I'll unify the inherent value of all layers using curve adjustment.

22
00:02:53,384 --> 00:02:54,280
How is it?

23
00:02:54,281 --> 00:02:55,960
The value's all broken now.

24
00:02:57,135 --> 00:03:03,160
If you decide to color with glazing, you have to work with the mindset of keeping that inherent value at least.

25
00:03:12,964 --> 00:03:16,280
This time, let me do the painting a bit more finely.

26
00:03:17,374 --> 00:03:21,251
First, I'll bring up the duplicate view and put it to the side.

27
00:03:58,915 --> 00:04:03,150
I'm tidying up the details of the clothing.

28
00:04:03,151 --> 00:04:08,270
Even with the same color clothing, if you bring out this kind of thickness like this, it can look like a different material.

29
00:04:10,015 --> 00:04:14,890
With a slight touch, the cloth-material clothing has turned into a material with a bit of cushion.

30
00:04:43,005 --> 00:04:47,400
Since I split the layers, I could paint easily.

31
00:04:47,401 --> 00:04:48,760
There are pros and cons.

32
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:52,280
I'm the type who merges layers as it gets to the later stages.

33
00:04:52,655 --> 00:04:57,000
But since I've split them well, I'll try to paint as much as possible without merging.

34
00:04:59,775 --> 00:05:03,571
I need to paint well aligned with the curvature of the body.

35
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:31,340
I'll also work on the face rendering, which I had applied carelessly.

36
00:06:31,341 --> 00:06:37,580
Rather than trying to draw a pretty face, I tend to try to draw an accurate face.

37
00:06:37,581 --> 00:06:41,100
Because I can't ignore the appeal that comes from realistic facial anatomy.

38
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:44,620
Have you heard of the uncanny valley effect?

39
00:06:44,621 --> 00:06:54,700
It's the phenomenon where as an object becomes more similar to a person, liking rises, but from a certain point it starts to look strange and unpleasant.

40
00:06:54,701 --> 00:07:02,840
For example, dolls or humanoid robots are likable, but clumsily human-like zombies or ghosts look unpleasant.

41
00:07:02,841 --> 00:07:07,560
In realistic-style drawing, realistic human depiction is very, very important.

42
00:07:07,561 --> 00:07:12,360
In the past, when drawing a smiling face, I didn't draw the nasolabial folds, or other things.

43
00:07:13,165 --> 00:07:17,880
Because I thought nasolabial folds are usually a factor that lowers the beauty.

44
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:23,000
But drawing like that, the face somehow looked unpleasant and strange.

45
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:28,080
These days, drawing with attention to human anatomy, I express all those parts too.

46
00:08:22,175 --> 00:08:26,010
I keep rendering.

47
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:26,490
A sci-fi style.

48
00:08:26,491 --> 00:08:31,130
Since it's a sci-fi style design, I plan to make the clothing a material with a very slight reflectance.

49
00:08:56,529 --> 00:08:57,530
That's why I'm giving it contrast.

50
00:08:57,531 --> 00:09:01,450
Shall we re-examine why the left part is brighter than the right part?

51
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:06,790
If you took Chapter 3, you'd have noticed right away.

52
00:09:06,791 --> 00:09:10,351
I've drawn a simple top-down view of this character.

53
00:09:10,715 --> 00:09:11,990
The light is coming from this side.

54
00:09:13,695 --> 00:09:22,531
The left part receives more light and reflects more toward me, so I drew it brighter.

55
00:10:43,905 --> 00:10:50,780
Because I usually sketch very briefly, after drawing to this extent, I delete the sketch layer and work.

56
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:56,220
But this time, since it's a sketch with a fair amount of detail rendering, it's a waste to get rid of it.

57
00:10:57,100 --> 00:10:59,500
I'll composite it into each color layer.

58
00:11:00,865 --> 00:11:03,420
We learned the layer shortcut in Chapter 3.

59
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:06,300
I won't mention it separately.

60
00:11:06,301 --> 00:11:12,981
For each color layer, I select the object and copy just that part of the sketch and merge it in.

61
00:11:14,815 --> 00:11:19,850
Honestly, there's not much difference between deleting the sketch layer and merging it like this.

62
00:11:19,851 --> 00:11:27,130
Because for the minimal rendering for 3D modeling, leftover sketch lines aren't a flaw.

63
00:11:27,131 --> 00:11:35,610
And in illustration, since every part of the picture gets painted over in the end, the sketch lines naturally disappear.

64
00:11:35,611 --> 00:11:39,930
The more you paint over, the more the sketch lines disappear and the picture looks more realistic.

65
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:49,550
It's good to save as you work, in the middle.

66
00:11:49,551 --> 00:11:53,630
I've already made four files from this picture.

67
00:11:53,631 --> 00:12:03,150
Once after sketching, once after overhauling the sketch, once at the first-pass coloring last time, and this is the file for the current picture.

68
00:12:03,151 --> 00:12:09,390
It also has the meaning of backup, but saving in the middle like this lets you compare and analyze.

69
00:12:09,391 --> 00:12:12,430
A picture doesn't always only go forward.

70
00:12:12,431 --> 00:12:14,670
It can go sideways, or even backward.

71
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:21,530
If you only keep looking at this picture, you have no way of knowing whether you're going sideways or backward.

72
00:12:21,531 --> 00:12:24,330
So you need a point of comparison.

73
00:12:24,331 --> 00:12:36,730
Compare with the picture I drew a few hours ago, or yesterday's picture, and bring back the parts where the previous picture was better, keep what to keep, and improve it.

74
00:12:36,731 --> 00:12:41,530
Likewise, I also leave multiple face layers.

75
00:12:41,610 --> 00:12:48,431
If I'm happily drawing along, then compare with the previous one and think the earlier face was better, I can easily revert to it.

76
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:57,900
I keep rendering.

77
00:12:57,901 --> 00:13:02,380
These are things I've explained many times, so I don't have much to say.

78
00:13:02,381 --> 00:13:09,420
As a side note, people usually say I speed up the video except for the parts I'm talking about during demos.

79
00:13:09,421 --> 00:13:16,700
But before filming the class, I did a survey, and there were many comments wanting to watch a video without speeding up.

80
00:13:17,925 --> 00:13:27,641
I'm not super fast with my hands, and I have a lot of trial and error while drawing, so if I film with no speeding up, it's too boring from the viewer's perspective.

81
00:13:27,710 --> 00:13:34,281
So I thought a lot, and figured maybe I could compromise with about a two or three times speed.

82
00:13:56,165 --> 00:13:59,200
I work on the face rendering in between too.

83
00:13:59,201 --> 00:14:03,360
It's important to render the whole thing evenly.

84
00:14:03,361 --> 00:14:06,320
I draw trying various brushes too.

85
00:14:06,321 --> 00:14:11,840
As you use various brushes, you'll figure out the brush that suits each situation.

86
00:14:11,841 --> 00:14:15,120
You'll find that brush very naturally.

87
00:14:15,121 --> 00:14:23,680
While you should be able to draw with just one default brush, that's the basic idea, and there's no need to use just one brush.

88
00:14:23,681 --> 00:14:25,920
It's good to use them flexibly.

89
00:14:28,055 --> 00:14:32,480
I remember in the past, coveting other people's brushes and collecting them.

90
00:14:32,481 --> 00:14:36,320
I thought if I used that artist's brush, I could draw like that artist.

91
00:14:37,150 --> 00:14:39,200
But that's not the case.

92
00:14:39,201 --> 00:14:45,520
Going into a famous chef's kitchen and cooking doesn't make my food as delicious as a first-rate chef's food.

93
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:51,040
In the end, whatever brush I use, pictures at my level come out.

94
00:14:52,465 --> 00:14:56,250
Then what standard should I use to collect brushes?

95
00:14:56,251 --> 00:15:00,410
As I said before, the brush that suits me is good, of course.

96
00:15:00,411 --> 00:15:09,530
Draw with a new brush, and if it feels good, if it feels like the picture is coming out well, collect that brush.

97
00:15:09,531 --> 00:15:15,770
It's not really that the picture comes out well because of the brush, but so you can keep going with that good feeling.

98
00:15:18,085 --> 00:15:23,120
To talk more about brushes, everyone exerts a different amount of hand strength when gripping a pen.

99
00:15:23,925 --> 00:15:30,000
In the past, when I was a job seeker, I used a very weak pressure level, so I drew exerting a lot of hand strength.

100
00:15:30,001 --> 00:15:32,640
It was to the point my fingers were crushed.

101
00:15:33,919 --> 00:15:39,721
Since I was young, I didn't know better, but now I've set it up so that even a little force produces a thick line.

102
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:45,050
I recommend holding the pen lightly and using it, if only for the sake of your wrists.

103
00:19:19,739 --> 00:19:23,321
If needed, I add to the sketch as I paint.

104
00:19:23,964 --> 00:19:25,320
I explained this many times.

105
00:19:26,069 --> 00:19:30,440
These sketch lines will be erased as I paint, so you don't have to worry about them.

106
00:19:32,379 --> 00:19:35,941
Honestly, even if sketch lines remain, they have their own charm.

107
00:19:37,294 --> 00:19:44,805
Due to the nature of digital drawing, it's easy to draw pictures without human touch, and you can think of it as a bit of humanity.

108
00:19:45,919 --> 00:19:50,160
These days, I tend to leave more sketch lines.

109
00:19:50,161 --> 00:19:51,520
It has charm as a picture.

110
00:23:59,949 --> 00:24:04,000
Painting a large area like this is quite a hassle.

111
00:24:04,001 --> 00:24:07,280
Too much touch can also look messy.

112
00:24:07,281 --> 00:24:11,120
Among the provided brushes, soft brushes can show their power at this time.

113
00:24:12,175 --> 00:24:17,671
It's good to set the direction of the folds with line drawing, then layer it in with a soft brush.

114
00:24:20,139 --> 00:24:26,951
When drawing folds in clothing, grasp the meeting points and let them fall naturally in the direction of gravity.

115
00:24:28,354 --> 00:24:32,190
This is easier said than done; drawing naturally is really hard.

116
00:24:33,529 --> 00:24:36,380
At this time, the best thing is copy-study.

117
00:24:36,381 --> 00:24:43,820
For example, if you hit a wall drawing clothing folds in this picture, find a photo of someone wearing a gown and draw it.

118
00:24:44,864 --> 00:24:50,820
After simply grasping the characteristics of the folds, come back to my picture and apply them.

119
00:24:51,774 --> 00:24:55,411
In my working method, I never draw with the reference up next to me.

120
00:24:55,879 --> 00:24:57,530
Copy-study is something you do separately.

121
00:29:33,069 --> 00:29:36,160
I'll also clean up the boundaries.

122
00:29:36,161 --> 00:29:41,600
When I draw, I think it's important to cleanly separate the boundaries between surfaces.

123
00:29:42,940 --> 00:29:45,020
Line drawing makes that easy.

124
00:29:46,569 --> 00:29:50,340
Even without lines, it's good to split the boundaries sharply.

125
00:30:28,600 --> 00:30:30,760
This is how I finished the first-pass work.

126
00:30:32,284 --> 00:30:36,600
From now on, it won't differ much from what the finished picture will look like.

127
00:30:36,601 --> 00:30:38,840
This is the start of boring work.

128
00:30:39,350 --> 00:30:41,160
They say you draw a picture with your glutes.

129
00:30:42,059 --> 00:30:45,681
Let me plant my glutes firmly and I'll see you next time.
